<h1>Characters</h1>
<p>A character controller is a mover built for walking, running, and jumping people and robots — not for crates. This page explains what <code>CharacterController3D</code> is, how it differs from a rigidbody, and every field and call you need to get a Skyforge pilot across a station deck.</p>

<h2>What CharacterController3D is</h2>
<p>Games need two very different kinds of moving objects. A crate should tumble, get shoved, and settle — the physics simulation owns it. A player should go exactly where the stick says, step over small ledges, stop at slopes that are too steep, and never tip over.</p>
<p><strong>CharacterController3D</strong> is the second kind. It is a component that moves an entity every fixed step using the collider shape, while resolving the details that make movement feel good: ground detection, step climbing, slope limits, and wall slide. It is not a rigidbody — nothing knocks it around, and it does not fall in a physically simulated way beyond the gravity you configure.</p>
<p>Use a character controller for players, bot pilots, and anything that should follow intent. Use a <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'rigidbodies'}) }}">Rigidbody3D</a> for props that belong to the simulation.</p>

<h2>When to use which</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>CharacterController3D</th><th>Rigidbody3D</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Responsive stick/keyboard movement</td><td>Yes</td><td>Only with heavy tuning</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Knockback, tumbling, stacking crates</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Step up small ledges automatically</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Slope limit (slide or stop on steep faces)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Friction only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pushed around by explosions</td><td>No (react in gameplay)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Driven by forces and mass</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A hybrid is allowed: the player uses <code>CharacterController3D</code>, and the crate the player drops uses <code>Rigidbody3D</code>. They collide normally through their colliders.</p>

<h2>Required collider setup</h2>
<p>A character controller drives a shape — it does not carry one. Validation enforces this exact arrangement:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Exactly one</strong> non-trigger collider on the same entity.</li>
  <li>The collider must be a primitive or convex shape: box, sphere, capsule, or convex hull. Capsule is the usual pick.</li>
  <li>No <code>Rigidbody3D</code> on that entity. A controller and a rigidbody on one body would fight over who moves it.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>{
  "CharacterController3D": {
    "slope_limit": 45,
    "step_height": 0.35,
    "skin_width": 0.02,
    "gravity": -18.0
  },
  "CapsuleCollider3D": {
    "radius": 0.35,
    "height": 1.7,
    "offset": [0, 0.85, 0],
    "is_trigger": false,
    "layer": "player"
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>The <code>CapsuleCollider3D</code> is the capsule the controller sweeps through the world.</li>
  <li><code>radius</code> and <code>height</code> size the capsule; <code>offset</code> <code>[0, 0.85, 0]</code> lifts it so the base sits at the entity origin (the feet).</li>
  <li><code>is_trigger: false</code> is required — a trigger shape cannot block movement.</li>
  <li><code>layer</code> participates in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-layers'}) }}">layer filtering</a> like any other collider.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Controller fields</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical value</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>slope_limit</code></td><td>Steepest walkable slope in degrees. Steeper faces are slid down rather than climbed.</td><td><code>45</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>step_height</code></td><td>Maximum ledge height climbed without jumping, in world units.</td><td><code>0.35</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>skin_width</code></td><td>Small gap kept between the shape and obstacles. Prevents sticking in corners and jitter against walls.</td><td><code>0.02</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gravity</code></td><td>Downward acceleration applied each step, world units per second squared. Negative pulls down.</td><td><code>-18.0</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Tuning notes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A <code>skin_width</code> too small lets the capsule catch on seams; too large and the character floats visibly above floors.</li>
  <li><code>step_height</code> above knee height makes stairs feel like ramps — usually not what you want.</li>
  <li><code>gravity</code> is gameplay feel, not planetary truth. Raise its magnitude for snappier jumps.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Driving the controller from Lua</h2>
<pre><code>local Character = require("demi.physics.character_controller3d")

Character.set_velocity(id, vx, 0, vz) -- horizontal intent; Y is physics-owned
local queued = Character.jump(id, 7) -- upward speed in world units/second

local state = Character.state(id)
-- state → table or nil: grounded, velocity { x, y, z }, ground_entity</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>set_velocity</code></td><td><code>id</code>, desired <code>vx, vy, vz</code></td><td>Boolean: <code>true</code> when the controller accepted the value. The current 3D movement step reads the horizontal X/Z values; gravity and jumping control Y.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>jump</code></td><td><code>id</code>, positive upward <code>speed</code> in world units/second</td><td>Boolean: <code>true</code> when the request was queued. The physics step applies it only when grounded; acceptance is not proof of a jump.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>state</code></td><td><code>id</code></td><td>Table with <code>grounded</code>, <code>velocity</code> <code>{ x, y, z }</code>, and <code>ground_entity</code>; <code>nil</code> without a controller.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A complete Skyforge pilot loop:</p>
<pre><code>local Character = require("demi.physics.character_controller3d")
local Input = require("demi.input")
local Player = {}

function Player:on_create()
    self.move_x, self.move_y = 0, 0
    self.jump_requested = false
end

function Player:on_update(dt)
    self.move_x, self.move_y = Input.vector("move")
    self.jump_requested = self.jump_requested or Input.pressed("jump")
end

function Player:on_fixed_update(fixed_dt)
    local state = Character.state(self.entity_id)
    if not state then return end
    local speed = 4.0
    Character.set_velocity(self.entity_id,
        self.move_x * speed, 0, self.move_y * speed)

    if self.jump_requested and state.grounded then
        Character.jump(self.entity_id, 7)
    end
    self.jump_requested = false
end

return Player</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>on_update</code> samples the action values and stores the jump edge so a fixed step can consume it.</li>
  <li><code>on_fixed_update</code> applies horizontal intent; the controller maintains vertical gravity and jump velocity.</li>
  <li><code>Character.state</code> reports grounded state and a one-based <code>velocity</code> array. <code>Character.jump</code> takes an upward speed; the grounded check prevents an airborne request from being discarded by the physics step.</li>
</ul>
<p>Details the controller handles for you:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wall slide</strong> — pushing into a wall moves along it instead of stopping dead.</li>
  <li><strong>Moving platforms</strong> — standing on a kinematic platform inherits its motion for the step.</li>
  <li><strong>Steps and slopes</strong> — ledges up to <code>step_height</code> are climbed; faces steeper than <code>slope_limit</code> are not.</li>
</ul>
<p>Trigger events still fire from the character’s shape, so zones notice the pilot (<a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-events'}) }}">Physics events</a>).</p>

<h2>Jump buffering and coyote time</h2>
<p>A press just before landing can be buffered in script state and consumed on a grounded fixed step. Keep the buffer on <code>self</code> so two characters do not share one timer:</p>
<pre><code>local Character = require("demi.physics.character_controller3d")
local Input = require("demi.input")
local BufferedJump = {}

function BufferedJump:on_create()
    self.jump_buffer = 0
end

function BufferedJump:on_update(dt)
    if Input.pressed("jump") then self.jump_buffer = 0.12 end
end

function BufferedJump:on_fixed_update(fixed_dt)
    local state = Character.state(self.entity_id)
    if state and state.grounded and self.jump_buffer > 0 then
        if Character.jump(self.entity_id, 7) then self.jump_buffer = 0 end
    else
        self.jump_buffer = math.max(0, self.jump_buffer - fixed_dt)
    end
end

return BufferedJump</code></pre>
<p><code>Character.jump</code> queues a request but the native step uses it only while grounded. Calling it during an airborne grace period does not produce coyote time. A true late jump off a ledge needs a separate movement policy; this controller API does not expose one.</p>

<h2>Character visuals</h2>
<p>The controller moves the entity; a <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'models-and-meshes'}) }}">model</a> supplies its visual. An <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'state-machines'}) }}">animation state machine</a> can read movement speed and grounded state. Add this method to the <code>Player</code> script above and call it after reading <code>state</code>:</p>
<pre><code>local Animation = require("demi.animation")

function Player:update_animation(state)
    local vx, vz = state.velocity[1], state.velocity[3]
    Animation.set_number(self.entity_id, "speed", math.sqrt(vx * vx + vz * vz))
    Animation.set_bool(self.entity_id, "grounded", state.grounded)
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>set_number</code> and <code>set_bool</code> feed state-machine parameters; call <code>Animation.trigger</code> only when a jump is actually started.</li>
  <li>Blend spaces pick idle/run from <code>speed</code>; transitions react to <code>grounded</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One non-trigger primitive or convex collider, no rigidbody.</li>
  <li>Capsule sized to the character with <code>offset</code> lifting it to the feet.</li>
  <li><code>slope_limit</code>, <code>step_height</code>, <code>skin_width</code>, and <code>gravity</code> tuned for the level’s stairs and ramps.</li>
  <li>Movement applied in <code>on_fixed_update</code> through <code>set_velocity</code>.</li>
  <li>Jump presses buffered in script when needed; native <code>jump</code> applies only while grounded.</li>
</ul>
